<p id="mwBA"><b id="mwBQ">Margaret Wooster Curti</b> (February 8, 1892 – September 19, 1961) was an American child psychologist, researcher and author.</p>

<p id="mwBg">Curti nee Wooster was born in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Silver_Creek,_Nebraska" title="Silver Creek, Nebraska" id="mwBw">Silver Creek, Nebraska</a> on February 8, 1892.<sup about="#mwt6" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Feminist_Voices_1-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Feminist Voices"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Feminist_Voices-1"}}'><a href="./Margaret_Wooster_Curti#cite_note-Feminist_Voices-1" id="mwCA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCg">[</span>1<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCw">]</span></span></a></sup> She attended the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./University_of_Nebraska–Lincoln" title="University of Nebraska–Lincoln" id="mwDA">University of Nebraska</a> where she studied inder <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Harry_Kirke_Wolfe" title="Harry Kirke Wolfe" id="mwDQ">Harry Kirke Wolfe</a>, and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./University_of_Chicago" title="University of Chicago" id="mwDg">University of Chicago</a> where she studied under <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Harvey_A._Carr" title="Harvey A. Carr" id="mwDw">Harvey A. Carr</a>, earning her PhD in 1920.<sup about="#mwt9" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ANB_2-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ANB"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-ANB-2"}}'><a href="./Margaret_Wooster_Curti#cite_note-ANB-2" id="mwEA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwEQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEg">[</span>2<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEw">]</span></span></a></sup> In 1925 she married fellow academic <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Merle_Curti" title="Merle Curti" id="mwFA">Merle Curti</a> with whom she had two children.</p>

<p id="mwFQ">She taught at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Smith_College" title="Smith College" id="mwFg">Smith College</a> from 1922 through 1937, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Teachers_College,_Columbia_University" title="Teachers College, Columbia University" id="mwFw">Teachers College, Columbia University</a> from 1937 through 1942, and then briefly at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./University_of_Wisconsin–Madison" title="University of Wisconsin–Madison" id="mwGA">University of Wisconsin–Madison</a>.<sup about="#mwt10" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ANB_2-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ANB"}}'><a href="./Margaret_Wooster_Curti#cite_note-ANB-2" id="mwGQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwGg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwGw">[</span>2<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwHA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwHQ">In 1930 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Longman" title="Longman" id="mwHg">Longmans, Green &amp; Co.</a> published her book <i id="mwHw">Child psychology</i>.<sup about="#mwt13" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-HathiTrust_3-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"HathiTrust"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-HathiTrust-3"}}'><a href="./Margaret_Wooster_Curti#cite_note-HathiTrust-3" id="mwIA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwIQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwIg">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwIw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwJA">Curti researched <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Cultural_bias" title="Cultural bias" id="mwJQ">cultural bias</a> in testing, disputing the idea that race or economic class effect a child's intelligence.<sup about="#mwt14" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ANB_2-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ANB"}}'><a href="./Margaret_Wooster_Curti#cite_note-ANB-2" id="mwJg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwJw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwKA">[</span>2<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwKQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwKg">Curti died in Madison, Wisconsin on September 19, 1961.<sup about="#mwt17" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Smith_College_Special_Collections_4-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Smith College Special Collections"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Smith_College_Special_Collections-4"}}'><a href="./Margaret_Wooster_Curti#cite_note-Smith_College_Special_Collections-4" id="mwKw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwLA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLQ">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwLw">Her papers are in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Sophia_Smith_Collection" title="Sophia Smith Collection" id="mwMA">Sophia Smith Collection</a> at Smith College.<sup about="#mwt18" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Smith_College_Special_Collections_4-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Smith College Special Collections"}}'><a href="./Margaret_Wooster_Curti#cite_note-Smith_College_Special_Collections-4" id="mwMQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwMg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMw">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

